# pkg/rpm/

RPM packaging templates for building an `.rpm` that includes:
- `@CLI_TOOL_NAME@` binary
- provisioning scripts for per-user sandbox accounts (sudoers wrapper, /home setup)

## Runtime dependencies

The `@CLI_TOOL_NAME@` package declares dependencies on:
- `systemd`
- `git`
- `ripgrep`
- `ncurses-libs`
- `ncurses`
- `sudo`
- `ca-certificates`
- `google-noto-sans-fonts`
- `chromium`

### Amazon Linux 2023 runtime dependency note

On Amazon Linux 2023, `ripgrep` and `chromium` are provided by Supplementary
Packages for Amazon Linux (SPAL), not by the default core repository. If `dnf`
cannot find those packages, update the host and enable SPAL before installing
`@CLI_TOOL_NAME@`:

```bash
sudo dnf upgrade
sudo dnf install spal-release
sudo dnf install ripgrep chromium
```

SPAL is available for AL2023 release version `2023.9.20251117` or later. You can
verify the host release with:

```bash
rpm -qi system-release
```

AWS documents SPAL as AL2023-compatible packages derived from EPEL9. SPAL
packages are provided separately from core AL2023 packages: they are not covered
by AWS Support Plans and do not receive AWS CVE tracking. See the AWS AL2023
documentation for details:

- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/linux/al2023/ug/spal.html
- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/linux/al2023/ug/configure-spal-repository.html

### CentOS Stream 10 runtime dependency note

On CentOS Stream 10, `ripgrep` and `chromium` may not be available from the
default enabled repositories. If `dnf` cannot find those packages, enable EPEL
before installing `@CLI_TOOL_NAME@`:

```bash
sudo dnf install epel-release
sudo dnf install ripgrep chromium
```

## Build dependencies

The RPM spec declares build dependencies on:
- `golang`
- `ncurses-devel`
- `pkgconfig`
- `make`
- `gcc`
- `git`
- `ca-certificates`
- `rpm-build`

### Go toolchain note (go1.26.2+)

This repo's `go.mod` currently requires Go 1.26.2.

- If your host has **Go 1.21+**, `go` can automatically download the required
  toolchain (when `GOTOOLCHAIN=auto`, which the packaging sets).
- If your distro `golang` is too old to bootstrap toolchain downloads, install
  a newer Go directly from https://go.dev/dl/ and rebuild.

Note: Some build environments restrict network access, which can prevent
automatic toolchain downloads.

## How to build locally

The recommended local build command is:

```bash
make rpm
```

This generates packaging templates, vendors Go dependencies, and runs the RPM
helper with RPM build-dependency checks skipped by default. This matches the
Debian local-build behavior, where developers may have Go/rpmbuild installed
outside the RPM package database.

To enforce the RPM `BuildRequires` checks instead:

```bash
make rpm RPMBUILD_FLAGS=
```

To run the helper directly, generate templates and vendor dependencies first:

```bash
make pkg-generate
make vendor
sh ./pkg/rpm/build.sh
```

The helper creates a temporary rpmbuild tree, archives the current source tree,
and runs `rpmbuild -bb` against `pkg/rpm/octium.spec`.

Built RPMs are copied to `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}` and printed by the helper.

If your build dependencies are installed outside your RPM database, you can skip
RPM dependency checks:

```bash
sh ./pkg/rpm/build.sh --nodeps
```

For CI/official builds, it's recommended to satisfy `BuildRequires` via your RPM
package manager.
